  "narration": "The chamber remains taut and airless after the earlier violence, every breath seeming too loud against the hush. Blood, restraint, and hard-won caution hang in the space between the prisoners and the crew, but no one moves to reopen the wound of the moment. Cold Tarn takes up his post with a clear, methodical watch, sweeping the perimeter and the Codex relay for any sign of interference while giving Sela the space to work. His calm warning, “Take your time, Sela. I’ve got overwatch,” settles into the scene like a steady hand on a shaking pulse.\n\nAcross the floor, the subdued operatives choose survival over pride. One pleads first, voice stripped of bravado: “Please, I’m not a threat. We’re wounded and unarmed—let us live, and I’ll cooperate.” Another follows with the same desperate bargain, asking not to be made to pay for a fight already lost. Their words are not resistance so much as an attempt to survive the consequences of having become prisoners.\n\nHard Vane answers that fragile stillness by stepping back from the edge of execution. He holsters his weapon, opens his hands, and makes his retreat from the lethal impulse unmistakable. “Your patients, Sela. I’ll cover the perimeter,” he says, shifting the burden of custody into discipline rather than threat. The gesture changes the shape of the room: the gunfire has already done its work, and now the choice before them is whether anything can be preserved after it.\n\nOathkeeper Sela moves through the aftermath with practiced urgency, medkit open, checking vitals and pressing the wounded back toward life. She works quickly—bandages, syn-skin foam, splints, careful hands and firm judgment—while telling the prisoners, “You’re going to survive this and face proper justice. That’s the statute I uphold.” The effort does not fully succeed, but it leaves a clear impression of intent: not vengeance, not surrender, but custody with a pulse. As she tends one body and then the next, the operatives’ pleas continue to thread through the hush, and the room ends in a tense, uneasy balance between mercy, law, and the memory of violence.",
